From November 2025 to July 2026 I was Tech Lead at Vitrina Online / NQLN ([nqlnstore.com](https://nqlnstore.com)), a retail store in Caracas that sells almost everything through WhatsApp. There we built **Necesitas Bot**, an assistant that serves customers in the same chat where they were already buying.

This article is for business owners who sell on WhatsApp and feel the chat is getting away from them. I'll cover the problem, what we built, the results we measured, and what I'd do the same (and differently) if I started today. At the end there's a short technical section for developers.

## The problem: customers waiting more than 7 hours

In Venezuela, a lot of people shop through WhatsApp. They write in, ask if a size or color is available, ask for the price in bolívares, send a screenshot of their Pago Móvil transfer, and wait for someone to confirm. For the store, each of those conversations is a possible sale.

The problem was that a person handled all of it, message by message. When we looked at the support panel, the average time to the **first human reply was 7 hours and 16 minutes**. And it wasn't for lack of people: about **10 people were answering the chat**.

Seven hours is a long time on WhatsApp. By then the customer has already written to another store, lost interest, or simply forgotten. On top of that, most questions were the same ones:

- Do you have this product? In what size?
- How much is it in bolívares?
- I already paid, can you confirm?
- Do you ship to my city? How much is it with MRW?

All of these have an answer in some system: the inventory, the day's exchange rate, the bank, the shipping table. Whoever was answering had to look up each piece of data, go back to the chat and type it. Multiply that by hundreds of conversations and you get 7 hours.

## What we built: Necesitas Bot

The idea was not a "press 1 for sales" menu. We wanted customers to write the way they always did, and get a fast answer based on real data.

Necesitas Bot does the following:

**It understands how people actually write.** It reads text messages, listens to voice notes and looks at photos. If a customer sends a photo of a product and asks "do you have this one?", the bot understands it.

**It checks real inventory.** It looks up stock in Shopify and Odoo, the systems where the store already kept its products. It doesn't make up availability: if something is out of stock, it says so.

**It validates payments.** The customer sends the receipt, including Pago Móvil with Banesco, and the bot checks the payment. This was one of the tasks that took the most time from the team.

**It handles currency and shipping.** It gives prices in the right currency, works out shipping (for example, with MRW), and builds the cart and the order inside the same conversation.

**It hands the chat to a person when it isn't sure.** This was key. When the bot isn't confident in its answer, or the customer has an unusual case, it passes the conversation to someone on the team. The customer doesn't get stuck going in circles with a machine that doesn't understand them.

**It works for several brands.** The company runs more than one brand, and each has its own tone and rules. The bot uses different instructions for each one, on the same foundation.

